Are you sure the disk has even a standard label on it to work with?
(what kind of an error do you get with just
#disklabel -r dsk1) OK, assuming /sbin is in your path and you have the
right disklabel program in that path...

If you don't have even a standard label, try:
#disklabel -wr dsk1 ##for the most basic bootable disk only. No
partitions have been marked as advfs yet. you'll need something like
this to get some advfs partitions cracking for you
:
#disklabel -Rr -t advfs /dev/rdisk/dsk1  # but this isn't what you
really, REALLY want. Once you get a standard label use the first
procedure, then you disklabel -e dsk1 and put in the numbers you have
above. That should do it. Any more questions, I'm happy to help.

Jack



